course: everyday
lesson: 215
title: Speaking up, saying no and growing up
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Bullies often say "Don't tell or it will be worse", because they rely on silence; telling a trusted adult is the way bullying stops.
explain: You can belong to a group without being mean to anyone, and you can say "No thanks" and walk away when someone offers a cigarette, a vape or alcohol, because they harm a growing body.
explain: Growing up means doing more things yourself: choosing layers for the weather, looking after pocket money, and talking with your parents about your own clothes or haircut within family rules.
explain: Pictures online show people's best moments, not their whole lives; real life has ups and downs for everyone.
explain: A girl in Denver is told by an older boy to keep quiet about him taking her lunch; she tells her teacher anyway, and the teacher makes it stop.
fact: Bullies rely on silence, so you tell a trusted adult even if you were told not to.
fact: You do not join in being mean to someone just to fit in with a group.
fact: If someone offers you a cigarette, a vape or alcohol, you say "No thanks" and walk away.
fact: Cigarettes, vapes and alcohol harm a growing body.
fact: Pictures online usually show only people's best moments.
fact: Saving some pocket money and spending some wisely means it does not run out all at once.
fact: Checking the weather and choosing layers helps you dress yourself to stay warm or cool.
fact: You can choose your own clothes or haircut by discussing it with your parents, within family rules.
fact: When you wonder why something happens, like why the sky is blue, you ask questions and look it up in books or with an adult.
fact: When you receive a gift, you say thank you and maybe write a card, to show you value the giver's kindness.
fact: When helping in the kitchen, you ask a grown-up for help with sharp knives and very hot water.
exercise: An older pupil takes your snack and says "If you tell anyone, it will be worse". What do you do? | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep quiet so it does not get worse | Give him your snack every day | Wait and hope that he gets bored
exercise: Why do bullies tell children to keep quiet? | Because they rely on silence to keep going | Because telling always makes bullying worse | Because teachers are not allowed to help | Because it is a rule of the playground
exercise: Your friends start calling a new boy names and look at you to join in. What do you do? | Do not join in, even if you want to fit in | Join in a little so you stay in the group | Laugh but do not say anything yourself | Join in, since he will not know it was you
exercise: An older teenager at the park offers you a vape. What do you do? | Say "No thanks" and walk away | Try it once so you are not left out | Take it and keep it for later on | Ask if it is a flavoured one first
exercise: Why should children say no to cigarettes, vapes and alcohol? | They harm a growing body | They are only bad for adults | They are fine if you have only a little | They only make your clothes smell
exercise: A girl scrolls online and feels everyone else has a perfect life. What is true? | Online pictures show only people's best moments | Other people really are happy all the time, every single day | Her life must be worse than everyone's | Pictures online always show real everyday life
exercise: You get ten dollars of pocket money. What is a wise plan? | Save some and spend some wisely | Spend it all on the very first day | Never spend any of it at all | Lend all of it to a friend
exercise: It is a cold, windy morning in Chicago and you are getting dressed by yourself. What do you do? | Check the weather and choose warm layers | Wear a T-shirt, since it is sunny indoors | Wear whatever you wore yesterday | Put on one very thick jumper only
exercise: Why are layers good when you dress for the weather? | You can add or take one off to stay comfortable | Layers are only for fashion | One thick layer is always warmer than several thinner ones | Layers stop you from ever getting cold
exercise: You want a very short haircut. What do you do? | Talk about it with your parents | Cut it yourself in the bathroom mirror | Ask a friend to cut it at school | Say nothing and hope they agree later
exercise: You wonder why the sky is blue. What is a good thing to do? | Ask questions and look it up with an adult or in books | Stop wondering, since it does not matter | Guess and decide your guess is true | Only ask your friends in the playground
exercise: Your aunt sends you a jigsaw for your birthday. What do you do? | Say thank you and maybe write her a card | Say nothing, since it only came through the post | Thank her only if you like it | Tell her you wanted a different one
exercise: You are helping wash up and there is a sharp knife in the sink. What do you do? | Ask a grown-up to wash the sharp knife | Feel for it carefully under all the bubbles | Leave it at the bottom of the sink | Wash it fast before anyone sees
exercise: Why should a child ask for help with hot water and knives in the kitchen? | Helping is useful but some things can hurt you | Children are not allowed to help in a kitchen at all | Hot water makes plates break | Knives get blunt when children wash them
test: A boy in Leeds is threatened: "Tell your mum and you're dead." What should he do? | Tell his mum or a teacher anyway | Keep the secret to stay safe | Fight the bully the next day | Change schools without saying why
test: Which is true about fitting in? | You can belong to a group without hurting others | To stay in a group you must always do whatever it does | Being mean once does not count | The leader of a group decides what is right
test: At a sleepover someone opens a can of beer and offers it round. What is the best answer? | "No thanks", and go and do something else | Take a small sip so that you seem a bit more grown up | Pretend to drink it | Drink it if nobody's parents will know
test: Maya feels ugly after looking at photos of a famous girl online. What would help her most? | Knowing online photos show only the best moments | Posting only perfect, edited photos of herself as well | Looking at more photos every day | Copying everything the famous girl wears
test: Sam spent all his birthday money in one afternoon and now wants a book. What lesson could he learn? | Saving some means the money lasts longer | Money always comes back if you wait | Books should be free for children | He should borrow from his sister every time
test: On a spring day in Dublin it is cold in the morning and warm later. What should you wear? | Layers you can take off as it warms up | One heavy winter coat all day, just to be safe | Only a T-shirt, since it will be warm | Whatever your friends are wearing
test: A girl in Perth wants to choose all her own school clothes. What is fair? | She chooses within the rules she agrees with her parents | She can wear anything she likes, always | Her parents must choose everything for her | She should copy her older sister exactly
test: Your uncle took you to the zoo and gave you a toy. Which shows real thanks? | Saying thank you and sending him a little card | Saying nothing, as he is family | Thanking him only for the toy | Asking for another toy next time
test: A child asks "Why do leaves fall in autumn?" and no adult knows. What is a good next step? | Look it up together in a book or online | Decide that nobody can ever know the answer to that | Make up an answer and believe it | Never ask such questions again
test: older kid at the park offered me a vape | Say no thanks and walk away | Try one puff just to be polite | Take it and save it for later | Say yes so that he'll like you more
test: they told me not to tell anyone they're picking on me | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep it secret, just as they said | Tell only the bullies' friends | Wait until it stops by itself
test: got £10 pocket money, spending all of it today on sweets | Save some and spend some wisely | Spend it all at once | Give it all away | Hide it and forget it
test: everyones life looks perfect online, mine's boring | Pictures online usually show only their best moments | Their lives really are perfect | Yours is boring for real | Post fake pictures too
explain: A boy in Texas got into a car with older boys who were speeding because he did not want to say no; the car crashed, and he broke his arm.
explain: A girl in Auckland was touched in a way that felt wrong by someone she knew, and she kept it secret for a long time; when she finally told her mum, the adults stopped it, and she wished she had told sooner.
fact: Saying no to something dangerous can feel hard, but going along to fit in is how many children get hurt.
fact: If anyone touches you in a way that feels wrong, it is never your fault; tell a trusted adult and keep telling until someone helps.
test: older kids want me in their car, driver's only 17 and goes mad fast | Say no and call your parent for a lift | Go so they think you're cool | Go but sit in the back | Go if it's a short trip
test: Someone tells Sam to keep a secret about touching that felt wrong. What should he do? | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep the secret as promised | Wait a few years | Tell only his friends
test: they're all smoking behind the shed and want me to try | Say no and walk away | Try one just to fit in | Pretend to smoke it a bit | Take one home for later
test: did u hear some year 6s were vaping behind the gym and one of them passed out and got rushed to hospital?? real lesson? | Vapes harm a growing body; say no thanks and walk away | Vaping is fine if you don't do too much | Only cigarettes are dangerous, not vapes | Try it once so you know what it's like
test: everyone's saying that influencer girl has the perfect life, look at her pics, my sister wants to be just like her. what's likely not real? | The perfect life; posts show only the best moments | That she posts photos online | That people like her pictures | That she has followers
test: apparently a kid told on the bullies and now they're gonna get him after school, so don't ever tell. what's the real lesson? | Tell a trusted adult anyway; bullies rely on silence | Never tell, it only makes things worse | Fight the bullies yourself instead | Join the bullies so they leave you alone
exam: Identify the appropriate response where a person offers a child a cigarette, a vape or alcohol. | To decline with «No thanks» and walk away | To accept but refrain from using it | To accept in order to fit in with the group | To keep it for later use
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises pictures posted online? | They usually depict only people's best moments | They invariably depict ordinary daily life as it truly is | They are always without alteration | They depict only unhappy moments
exam: Select the response consistent with established guidance where a child is touched in a manner that feels wrong. | It is never his fault; he informs a trusted adult and persists until helped | He keeps it secret to avoid trouble | It is his fault if he did not refuse | He tells only another child
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which a child should not be silent about bullying. | Bullies rely upon silence to continue | Bullying ceases sooner if it is ignored | Adults are not permitted to intervene in it | Telling invariably causes more bullying
